# Projective quantum measurement, the measurement postulate of quantum mechanics, modeled and proved by the kernel. The
# postulate says a measurement in a basis is a set of projectors, one per outcome, that are mutually orthogonal and sum
# to the identity. Applying the projector for an outcome PICKS OUT the part of the state lying along that outcome and
# discards the rest. For a single qubit in the computational basis there are exactly two outcomes and two projectors,
# P0 onto the zero state and P1 onto the one state. Three algebraic laws define a projective measurement, and all three
# hold regardless of the complex amplitudes, so they are exactly checkable on the discrete basis states.
#   IDEMPOTENCE: P P = P. Measuring the same outcome twice changes nothing. A projector applied again does nothing new.
#   ORTHOGONALITY: P0 P1 = 0. Distinct outcomes exclude each other. Projecting onto one outcome then the other vanishes.
#   COMPLETENESS: P0 + P1 = identity. Some outcome always occurs. Every basis state is kept by exactly one projector.
# This file models the projectors as functions ON BASIS STATES, the discrete and faithful core of the postulate. A
# projector either KEEPS a basis state (when it matches the outcome) or sends it to the NULL vector (when it does not).
# That is the full action of a rank-one computational-basis projector on a basis vector, with no amplitude lost. It
# CONSTRUCTS the basis states and the projection result, COMPUTES the two projectors, and PROVES idempotence,
# orthogonality, and completeness by induction over the basis state.

load @term/seed/code/atom/flag
  find flag

# The two computational-basis states of one qubit, the zero state and the one state. These are the outcomes a basis
# measurement can resolve, and the eigenvectors the two projectors keep.
form basis
  case zero-state
  case one-state

# The result of applying a projector to a basis state. A computational-basis projector is rank one, so on a basis vector
# it either KEEPS that vector unchanged (the outcome matched) or sends it to the NULL vector (the outcome did not match).
# vanish is the null vector, the zero of the space, what an orthogonal projector returns. keep carries the surviving
# basis state. These two cases are the entire image of a rank-one projector acting on a basis vector.
form result
  case vanish
  case keep
    link state, like basis

# Apply P0, the projector onto the zero state, to a basis state. It KEEPS the zero state and VANISHES the one state.
task project-zero
  take s, like basis
  like result
  fork case, read s
    case zero-state
      send back
        make keep
          bind state
            make zero-state
    case one-state
      send back
        make vanish

# Apply P1, the projector onto the one state, to a basis state. It KEEPS the one state and VANISHES the zero state, the
# exact complement of P0.
task project-one
  take s, like basis
  like result
  fork case, read s
    case zero-state
      send back
        make vanish
    case one-state
      send back
        make keep
          bind state
            make one-state

# Apply P0 to a RESULT rather than a fresh basis state, so projectors can be composed. The null vector stays null
# (projecting nothing gives nothing), and a kept basis state is re-projected by P0. This lets P0 P0 and P1 P0 be written
# as one expression and folded over.
task project-zero-on-result
  take r, like result
  like result
  fork case, read r
    case vanish
      send back
        make vanish
    case keep
      link state
      send back
        call project-zero
          read state

# Apply P1 to a RESULT, the companion of project-zero-on-result. The null vector stays null, and a kept basis state is
# re-projected by P1. With this, P0 P1, P1 P1, and the orthogonality composition are expressible and checkable.
task project-one-on-result
  take r, like result
  like result
  fork case, read r
    case vanish
      send back
        make vanish
    case keep
      link state
      send back
        call project-one
          read state

# Which single outcome a basis state belongs to, yes for the zero state and no for the one state. Used to state
# completeness as a partition: each basis state is claimed by exactly one of the two projectors.
task is-zero-outcome
  take s, like basis
  like flag
  fork case, read s
    case zero-state
      send back
        make yes
    case one-state
      send back
        make no

# Whether a result KEEPS some basis state (an outcome occurred) rather than vanishing. Completeness says that across the
# two projectors a basis state is kept exactly once, so the two keep-flags are complementary.
task is-kept
  take r, like result
  like flag
  fork case, read r
    case vanish
      send back
        make no
    case keep
      link state
      send back
        make yes

# IDEMPOTENCE OF P0: project-zero-on-result (project-zero s) = project-zero s, for every basis state. Applying P0 twice
# equals applying it once, P0 P0 = P0. Measuring the zero outcome a second time changes nothing. Proved by induction
# over the basis state.
rule project-zero-is-idempotent
  mark s, like basis
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call project-zero
        read s
        link project-zero-on-result
      call project-zero
        read s
  fold s

# IDEMPOTENCE OF P1: project-one-on-result (project-one s) = project-one s, for every basis state. P1 P1 = P1, the same
# involution-free stability for the one outcome. Proved by induction over the basis state.
rule project-one-is-idempotent
  mark s, like basis
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call project-one
        read s
        link project-one-on-result
      call project-one
        read s
  fold s

# ORTHOGONALITY P1 AFTER P0: project-one-on-result (project-zero s) = vanish, for every basis state. P1 P0 = 0. Whatever
# P0 keeps lies entirely along the zero state, which P1 annihilates, so the composition always reaches the null vector.
# Distinct outcomes exclude each other. Proved by induction over the basis state.
rule project-zero-then-one-vanishes
  mark s, like basis
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call project-zero
        read s
        link project-one-on-result
      make vanish
  fold s

# ORTHOGONALITY P0 AFTER P1: project-zero-on-result (project-one s) = vanish, for every basis state. P0 P1 = 0, the other
# order. Orthogonality is symmetric: either composition of the two projectors annihilates. Proved by induction over the
# basis state.
rule project-one-then-zero-vanishes
  mark s, like basis
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call project-one
        read s
        link project-zero-on-result
      make vanish
  fold s

# COMPLETENESS AS A PARTITION: for every basis state, the state is kept by P0 exactly when it is the zero outcome, and is
# kept by P1 exactly when it is NOT the zero outcome. is-kept (project-zero s) = is-zero-outcome s. So each basis state
# is kept by exactly one of the two projectors, never both and never neither, which is P0 + P1 = identity read on basis
# vectors. Proved by induction over the basis state.
rule p-zero-keeps-exactly-the-zero-outcome
  mark s, like basis
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call project-zero
        read s
        link is-kept
      call is-zero-outcome
        read s
  fold s

# The opposite basis state, the swap zero-state to one-state and back. Used to phrase the P1 completeness half as the
# negation of the zero outcome.
task other-outcome
  take s, like basis
  like basis
  fork case, read s
    case zero-state
      send back
        make one-state
    case one-state
      send back
        make zero-state

# The complementary half of completeness: P1 keeps a basis state exactly when it is NOT the zero outcome. is-kept
# (project-one s) = is-zero-outcome s is FALSE in general, so instead we state that P1 keeps exactly the one outcome,
# the negation of the zero outcome. Read with the rule above, the two projectors keep complementary sets whose union is
# every basis state, the partition that is completeness. Proved by induction over the basis state.
rule p-one-keeps-exactly-the-one-outcome
  mark s, like basis
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call project-one
        read s
        link is-kept
      call is-zero-outcome
        call other-outcome
          read s
  fold s

# CONCRETE WITNESS THAT THE TWO PROJECTORS ARE DISTINCT: on the zero state P0 keeps it while P1 vanishes it. The two
# projectors disagree on at least one basis state, so they are genuinely different operators, not the same projector
# twice. Witnessed by computation.
rule projectors-differ-on-the-zero-state
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call project-zero
        make zero-state
        link is-kept
      make yes
  calm hold

rule p-one-vanishes-the-zero-state
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call project-one
        make zero-state
      make vanish
  calm hold
